<Base title="ABN, TFN and WWVP — Career Central">
  <h1>Getting set up: ABN, TFN and WWVP</h1>
  <p>
    Three things come up before almost any first job, contract gig, or
    volunteering role involving children or vulnerable people. All three are
    free (or near enough) to get directly from the government — never pay a
    third-party site to "apply" for you.
  </p>

  <h2>TFN — Tax File Number</h2>
  <p>
    Your personal tax identifier, used for the rest of your life. You need
    one before you start any paid job, casual or otherwise.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Apply free through the ATO (Australian Taxation Office) online, by post, or in person — not through a paid agency.</li>
    <li>
      Once you have a job, your employer gives you a <strong
        >TFN declaration</strong
      > to complete — do this in your first week. Skip it and you'll be taxed
      at the top marginal rate on every dollar until you provide it.
    </li>
    <li>If you have two jobs, only claim the tax-free threshold on the one paying you more.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>ABN — Australian Business Number</h2>
  <p>
    An identifier for running a business — sole-trader freelancing, tutoring
    independently, content/creative gigs invoiced directly, that kind of
    thing. <strong>You do not need one to be a regular employee</strong>,
    casual included.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Apply free via the Australian Business Register (the ABR) — you'll need a TFN first.</li>
    <li>
      If an employer asks you to get an ABN so they can pay you as a
      "contractor" for what is really an ordinary job — set hours, their
      equipment, told how to do the work — that can be <strong
        >sham contracting</strong
      >, which is against the law. It shifts tax and super obligations onto
      you that should be theirs. If this happens, that's worth bringing to a
      query.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>WWVP — Working with Vulnerable People registration</h2>
  <p>
    The ACT's background check for anyone working or volunteering with
    children, older people, or people with disability — required for a lot of
    tutoring, mentoring, and camp-style volunteering roles that also count
    toward <a href="/guides/volunteering/">ANU+</a>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Apply through Access Canberra; it includes a national police check and is free for volunteer (unpaid) roles, and low-cost for paid ones.</li>
    <li>Processing can take a few weeks, so apply as soon as you know a role will need it — not the week before you start.</li>
    <li>Registration is valid for three years and covers all your WWVP-eligible roles in that time, so you don't need a fresh one per role.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Something about an offer or a form doesn't add up — sham contracting,
    being asked to pay for a "registration service", anything like that?{" "}
    <a href="/">Book a query</a> and get a second set of eyes on it.
  </p>
</Base>
